WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Oh god, oh god, oh god. I’m trapped in the&nbsp;
Infinite Ikea! How long have I been here?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.880
How can I get out? How can I survive&nbsp;
the vicious Staff of the Infinite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.720
Ikea and work with the other survivors&nbsp;
in this terrifying, endless building?

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:17.840
Okay. Let’s start from the beginning.
Day 1. I’d come to this flat-pack nightmare with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:23.360
my lovely wife, Brenda, to pick up some stupid&nbsp;
sofa she saw online. We could have ordered it in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.240
but me, being a cheapskate and a fool,&nbsp;
instead decided it’d be a dandy idea to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.600
head in and pick it up ourselves - even though&nbsp;
I can’t stand shopping in these giant stores.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.600
Of course, it didn’t take long for us to&nbsp;
get separated. I was wandering around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.240
just pretending I knew what I was doing.&nbsp;
Surrounded by unfamiliar people. Then…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.360
Not surrounded by any people at all. Like the&nbsp;
complete doofus I was, I’d somehow gotten lost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.280
Just needed to find my bearings again and&nbsp;
then I could call Brenda to come save me.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:59.040
But I never did find my bearings. The&nbsp;
hours went on, and I was still lost…&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.120
Day 2. My dominant emotion on&nbsp;
this day was nothing more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.800
sheer humiliation, knowing I’d been bested by&nbsp;
a damn Swedish furniture store. I spent the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.920
night before sleeping on a futon, wondering how&nbsp;
I’d gotten myself into this flat-pack calamity.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.840
I spent the day searching for food,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.160
my confusion and exhaustion increasing by&nbsp;
the moment. For a while, I even entertained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.520
the idea I might have died and gone to some&nbsp;
Nordic hell. That night, I went to bed hungry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.000
knowing that if I didn’t eat soon, I might&nbsp;
be found as a skeleton on a dusty old futon.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:37.360
It can’t end like this. I can’t die on day 2…
Day 3. I continued my journey through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.800
labyrinthine bowels of the Ikea, disoriented&nbsp;
by the endlessly iterating collections of cheap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.680
furniture. There was something terrifying about&nbsp;
the emptiness of it all, this affordable but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.960
impossible to assemble void. Starvation has always&nbsp;
been one of the most horrific deaths, hasn’t it?

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.120
You could only imagine my relief when I saw&nbsp;
the figure standing a few feet in front of me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.160
dressed like a member of Ikea staff. I’d found&nbsp;
salvation! I’d found someone who could help me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.120
out of here! But when I approached, I&nbsp;
realized something was horribly wrong:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:12.320
This wasn’t a human being standing before&nbsp;
me, it was a monster. A being I’d later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:16.480
come to know is called The Staff&nbsp;
by the many people who fear them.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:22.240
It chased me, repeating, “The store is closed. You&nbsp;
need to vacate the premises”, flailing for me with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:27.840
its long, frightening limbs. I only survived Day&nbsp;
3 because I locked myself in a closet and just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.240
waited while it hammered against the wood&nbsp;
with its fists. Once the “night” was over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:38.080
it left, and I was able to escape.
Day 4. I was feeling some intense hunger pangs on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.800
this day, not to mention the fact that I now knew&nbsp;
there were monsters out there, just waiting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:48.000
beat me to death if they caught me when the lights&nbsp;
turned down. Needless to say, I wasn’t in the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.760
headspace, and I didn’t have enough charge on my&nbsp;
phone to justify opening up my meditation app.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:59.760
Then… I found Nirvana. I found the&nbsp;
cafeteria, stocked full of delicious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.880
warm Swedish meatballs. No food&nbsp;
had ever tasted so sweet to me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.880
And this delicious meal also gave way to one&nbsp;
of the most exciting new developments: Gloria.&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.120
Gloria was a veteran. She taught me&nbsp;
everything I needed to know at this place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.280
Even on the first day I met her, she felt like&nbsp;
someone I’d known for years. It was her that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:24.000
took me back to her home in this place: A little&nbsp;
fortress made of Ikea furniture, filled with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.040
whole community of other people trapped in there.&nbsp;
It was like being allowed into the Garden of Eden.&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.800
Day 5, and it feels good to be alive.&nbsp;
I met up with all the different people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.320
around the camp. They tell the most bizarre&nbsp;
and fascinating stories. This sounds crazy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.360
I know, but I get the sense not all of them&nbsp;
came from the same place as me. Different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:50.800
Ikeas in different countries, or maybe even -&nbsp;
as nutty as it sounds - from different worlds.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.600
One guy, Tony, who’s been trapped in here for&nbsp;
a year and change, and we got talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.600
different vacations we’d taken on the outside.&nbsp;
He told me he was from New York, and I told him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.160
I visited there once, and I loved going to see&nbsp;
the Statue of Liberty up close. That’s when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:09.520
told me that he’d never heard of the Statue of&nbsp;
Liberty. I didn’t know what to make of that.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:13.680
Strange little details aside, I couldn’t&nbsp;
be happier to be there with other people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.960
The next step would be finding a&nbsp;
way out of here, and back to Brenda.&nbsp;

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:21.600
Day 6. Gloria and several others led me&nbsp;
out on our first excursion - Missions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.280
where the goal was to collect more food&nbsp;
and supplies, and map the surrounding area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:29.440
I nearly jumped out of my skin when I saw a&nbsp;
Staff member standing in our path at one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:34.400
and the others all just laughed at me. The&nbsp;
Staff member just stood there, placid and still.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:39.360
Gloria told me that it’s okay, the Staff are&nbsp;
harmless during the day. It’s only nighttime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.520
when they enter their pattern of aggression. So&nbsp;
as long as you don’t get lost during the daytime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.360
you’re generally fine. The team&nbsp;
often used string, like Theseus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:51.680
to trail behind them and ensure they don’t get&nbsp;
lost. It was clear they’d been here long enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:55.760
to work out systems for every possibility.&nbsp;
Felt like I was in good hands with them.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:59.840
We collected meatballs and some rugs&nbsp;
to fortify the walls and headed back.&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:06.240
Day 7. This was the first night that we had to&nbsp;
fend off a full-on attack. Those monsters, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:12.400
Staff, came at us in huge groups, pounding at the&nbsp;
outside of our perimeter with their balled fists.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.560
It was terrifying. As a way of fighting&nbsp;
them off, we tied Ikea kitchen knives to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.920
the end of curtain rails and speared them,&nbsp;
one by one, until all of them were dead.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:26.560
But they just kept coming. More and more and&nbsp;
more of them. When it looked like one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.400
walls to the north of the community was going&nbsp;
to fall, everyone around me started to panic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:34.400
That’s when Barry, one of the biggest men in&nbsp;
the camp, grabbed a hammer in each hand and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:39.440
when outside. He fought like a beast,&nbsp;
taking on Staff member after Staff member,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.400
tanking hit after hit. It was something to see.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.640
That’s when he took off into&nbsp;
the depths of the store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:49.440
drawing the Staff away behind him and saving&nbsp;
us all. We never saw Barry again after that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:54.400
but he’s the reason all of us made it past&nbsp;
day 7. Thank you, Barry, wherever you are.&nbsp;

00:05:54.400 --> 00:06:00.080
Day 8. Gloria took me out alone today, on another&nbsp;
search for the escape. That’s when she told me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.320
about her sister: She’d gone shopping in&nbsp;
Ikea with her well over a year ago now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:09.360
when she was separated and got lost in here,&nbsp;
just like me. I had a lot in common with her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.320
including my feelings of guilt for abandoning&nbsp;
my loved ones and my drive to escape and be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:18.400
united with them. While we were out that&nbsp;
day, we didn’t find anything useful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:24.720
Gloria seemed sad but unsurprised. The Infinite&nbsp;
Ikea had its way of slowly grinding you down.&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:31.200
Days 9 to 17. Despite a rocky start, I was finding&nbsp;
my legs in the Infinite Ikea. I started to get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.160
know my fellow Ikea prisoners, I started to&nbsp;
understand and truly befriend them. We went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:40.320
on expeditions pretty much daily, either&nbsp;
to collect new food, more supplies to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:44.560
build up our community, or to keep searching&nbsp;
for an exit. To me, it started to feel like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:49.840
we were making progress, and that helped a&nbsp;
great deal to keep my emotions semi-stable.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:54.080
But it wasn’t the same for Gloria. After all,&nbsp;
she’d been here for so much longer than me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:59.680
and she had her sister to consider on the outside.&nbsp;
To her, these routines I was becoming part of now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:04.400
felt like a prison within the prison. She&nbsp;
was trapped. Had her sister forgotten her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:08.800
out there? Had she been declared dead?&nbsp;
Were people even still looking for her?

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.440
And it was on Day 18 that it all got&nbsp;
a little too much for poor Gloria.&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:18.000
She’d snuck out of the camp at night, when the&nbsp;
staff were most active, trying to find the exit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:24.080
Sadly, that’d cost Gloria her life. There’s no&nbsp;
way of knowing what happened to her exactly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:27.760
but considering how bruised up&nbsp;
her body was when we found her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:32.240
it was easy to make an educated guess: She’d&nbsp;
gotten bum-rushed by the Staff and beaten to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:37.200
death before she could even muster up a defense.&nbsp;
It was a horrible day to go. We tried to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:43.040
her as dignified a funeral as we could, given the&nbsp;
circumstances: Closing her up in a body-sized box.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:48.320
That was the day I decided to stop just&nbsp;
trying to survive, and start trying to escape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:53.120
I owed that to Brenda, if she really had&nbsp;
gotten out. I couldn’t keep her waiting.&nbsp;

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:58.240
But I wouldn’t be alone. As it turns out,&nbsp;
another two members of the little Ikea community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:02.000
I’d come to know were willing to risk&nbsp;
it all with me: A man named Kelvin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:06.000
and a young woman named Vicky. They were&nbsp;
sick of just waiting around and fending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.680
off attacks from the Staff, night after&nbsp;
night. They both told me they’d rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:14.000
die during an escape attempt than while&nbsp;
cowering under a pile of cheap rugs.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.560
And so, each armed with claw hammers&nbsp;
from the Six-Piece Ikea Fixa tool kit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:22.480
and as many Pruta Tupperware containers&nbsp;
full of meatballs as we could carry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:25.680
we set off into the great unknown… of Ikea.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.520
We traveled for weeks, marking out&nbsp;
tracks on the ground with the MÅLA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:33.280
mixed-colors chalk selection so we&nbsp;
never got caught going in circles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:38.800
One day bled into the next: Nights were spent&nbsp;
trying to hide in closets and bathtub while the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:44.320
Staff hunted relentlessly for people just&nbsp;
like us. Every single time, we got lucky.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.920
Until day 41.
Here’s something you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:52.480
to know about the Infinite Ikea: You’re probably&nbsp;
already aware, if you’re watching this, that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:58.000
24-hour cycle of night and day is dictated by the&nbsp;
store lights up above. But the space between day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:04.080
and night isn’t a gradient here, it’s a cliff. You&nbsp;
can be minding your own business, when suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:09.600
pitch darkness, and now the staff are on your&nbsp;
ass. That’s exactly what happened on Day 41.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:13.760
We were in the middle of a kitchenware section,&nbsp;
surrounded by a few docile members of staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:19.680
when suddenly, the lights switched off and they&nbsp;
went hostile on us. They look pretty goofy after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:24.480
the initial shock has worn off, but believe me&nbsp;
when I say that these monsters can really pack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:29.760
a mean wallop when they want to, and we received&nbsp;
a reminder of this unfortunate fact that night.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:35.280
The staff swarmed us, repeating that awful&nbsp;
phrase, “The store is closed. You need to vacate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:40.000
the premises”, while they struck and flailed at&nbsp;
us. If it wasn’t for our trusty claw hammers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:44.560
we would have been dead that night. Thankfully,&nbsp;
we were able to give better than we were getting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:48.080
We managed to kill a decent number&nbsp;
of staff members and then make a run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:52.720
for a section with better hiding places.&nbsp;
Myself, Vicky, and Kelvin all stowed away in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:57.040
a large wardrobe until we saw light filtering&nbsp;
through the crack in the door, like we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:02.160
rejected extras for some painful community&nbsp;
theatre take on The Chronicles of Narnia.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:06.960
But while we survived that night, we didn’t&nbsp;
survive unscathed. My face was swollen from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:11.440
a nasty punch one of the staff members&nbsp;
dealt me, and from the pain in my chest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:16.480
I might’ve been dealing with a few broken ribs.&nbsp;
Kelvin sprained his ankle during the escape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:20.080
and Vicky had a cut on her forehead from&nbsp;
when one of the staff members kneed her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:24.800
in the face during the fray. You never&nbsp;
win these fights, you just survive them.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:28.320
We made a temporary camp in the area&nbsp;
where we could rest and recover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:32.320
as well as shaking off the justifiable&nbsp;
fear of death or grievous harm that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:37.280
dampened our resolve to get out of&nbsp;
this place. That took us to day 53.&nbsp;

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:41.840
Of course, food was always a concern. I don’t&nbsp;
want to romanticize what happened in there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:46.400
as much as I’m sure someone on the outside might&nbsp;
want to imagine this whole experience as some kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:52.000
of exciting, survival horror game. But I assure&nbsp;
you, it was less “survival horror” and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.960
survival and horror. Meaning not only are&nbsp;
we suffering from constant fear, stress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:01.440
and paranoia for our safety in here, but we&nbsp;
also need to keep ourselves fed and watered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:05.920
You’re just as likely to die from starvation&nbsp;
in here as you are to be beaten to death.

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:09.680
We went in search of another Ikea Kitchen,&nbsp;
where we could fill up on more water and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:14.080
meatballs - your lifeblood in a place&nbsp;
like this. It took us several more days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:19.440
of searching and hiding, searching and&nbsp;
hiding before we hit paydirt. By the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:23.680
time we actually got our hands on the food&nbsp;
and water, we were starving and practically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:28.640
coughing up dust. Those meatballs were&nbsp;
the most delicious food I’ve ever tasted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:32.960
and I could tell from their faces that&nbsp;
Kelvin and Vicky felt exactly the same way.

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:38.560
Though at that moment, I told myself,&nbsp;
if - no, when - I get out of here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:45.200
I’d never eat another meatball. It’d probably give&nbsp;
me war flashbacks - or, I guess, store flashbacks.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:50.000
We filled up our Tupperware and shoved them back&nbsp;
into our Ikea Pivring backpacks. Then, we needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:54.960
to keep moving, keep searching, and keep marking&nbsp;
the ground behind us as we fanned out into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.960 --> 00:12:00.320
great flat-pack yonder, avoiding confrontations&nbsp;
whenever we could. The three of us still had no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:05.200
idea what insanity was waiting for us out there.&nbsp;
We had no idea that there were even more dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:10.720
things than the Staff lurking in the shadows.
Day 68. Of course, I kept count, writing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.040
down in my on the back of a JÄTTELIK coloring&nbsp;
book. Trust me, when every night could mean a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:21.280
horrible death, you keep track of the nights. Not&nbsp;
a single one of them escapes you. At a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:26.800
point, I think we all adapted, in our own way.&nbsp;
It was back to caveman times again, learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:32.000
to be like our primal ancestors, hiding away&nbsp;
from the dark and the monsters that hid there.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:37.520
So it was extremely surprising for us to&nbsp;
get the most brutal attack during the day.

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:41.200
At first, I thought we were being attacked&nbsp;
by the staff during daylight hours, like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:45.680
bolt from the blue. That’s when we noticed&nbsp;
they weren’t attacking with their hands:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:50.480
They were all holding kitchen knives, holding&nbsp;
us up like bandits. That’s when we realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:55.840
what had actually happened here: We weren’t being&nbsp;
attacked by the staff, we were being attacked by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.840 --> 00:13:01.200
other humans dressed like the staff, wearing&nbsp;
their hollowed-out heads like grisly masks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:05.840
They told us that we were coming with them, and&nbsp;
if we resisted, they’d cut us to ribbons. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:11.920
seeing as none of us were movie action heroes, we&nbsp;
thought it’d be best to do exactly what they said.

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:16.560
This was how we fell into the&nbsp;
clutches of Generalissimo Vardagen.

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:25.200
Day 69, but things were not nice. I don’t&nbsp;
know if I mentioned this before, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:30.000
community I became a part of in the Infinite&nbsp;
Ikea after meeting Gloria was just one of many.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.000
Nobody knows exactly how many people&nbsp;
are trapped in here. I’m hardly a martyr&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:38.400
for spending 68 days in there, I know people&nbsp;
who’ve been trapped in there for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:41.680
who’ve given up all hope of escape&nbsp;
and accepted their lot in life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:45.840
They became the elders of a lot of these&nbsp;
communities, helping others adjust.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:50.400
Though of course, Generalissimo&nbsp;
Vardagen was not one of these people.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:55.120
Myself, Vicky, and Kelvin were dragged by the&nbsp;
strangers dressed as staff members into a fort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.120 --> 00:14:00.720
made of smashed-up wood, nailed together into&nbsp;
a huge, ominous structure. It was a far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:05.440
extensive structure than any of the communities&nbsp;
I’d visited or even heard about before in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:11.680
Infinite Ikea. It was a true fortress, guarded&nbsp;
by many more of those knife-wielding people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:15.600
dressed in the clothes and the flesh&nbsp;
of the staff. They looked like some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:20.320
evil cult straight out of a damn horror&nbsp;
movie; I’d never seen anything like it.

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:23.440
We were dragged into a kind of&nbsp;
tent in the middle of the camp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:28.640
made out of stitched-together rugs.&nbsp;
That’s where we met Generlissimo Vardagen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:33.360
surrounded by his guards. I’d later learn his&nbsp;
namesake was a set of steak knives stocked in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:38.400
the Ikea kitchenware sections - similar to the&nbsp;
knives being wielded by his gallery of goons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:42.880
The Generalissimo himself was dressed&nbsp;
like some absurd tinpot dictator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:48.640
wearing a silly hat and a jacket covered in fake&nbsp;
medals. His whole presence felt like a cosmic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:53.120
punishment for daring to believe things couldn’t&nbsp;
get any more absurd than they already were.

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:58.000
His men forced us down onto our knees,&nbsp;
and Vardagen cleared his throat to speak.&nbsp;

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:06.000
“Quake in fear at the sight of this Ikea’s ruler,&nbsp;
the great and powerful Generalissimo Vardagen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:12.080
I have united kingdoms from the gardening and&nbsp;
bathroom supplies departments, and crushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:20.160
dissenting tribes in the office furniture section&nbsp;
to the West. If you wish to live, you will swear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:26.800
fealty to me and join my legion of servants.&nbsp;
If you do as I say, you will be given safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:35.200
and security from the staff come nightfall. If you&nbsp;
do not bend the knee, I will have you destroyed!”

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:39.680
By the time his little spiel was done, the man&nbsp;
was red in the face and sweating profusely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:44.000
It was clear that, much like your average Ikea&nbsp;
shelving unit after a couple weeks of use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:49.200
the great Generalissimo Vardagen had a few&nbsp;
screws loose. None of us liked the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:55.440
becoming slaves to some flat-pack ghenghis khan,&nbsp;
so we tried to persuade him to just let us go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.440 --> 00:16:00.240
telling him that we hoped to escape the store,&nbsp;
and with our help, he could escape, too.

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:06.080
That’s when we learned a sobering lesson: For&nbsp;
some people, life inside the Infinite Ikea was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:10.720
better than life outside. In the real world,&nbsp;
the Generalissimo had been a twice-divorced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:16.960
ex-salaryman with nothing to his name but&nbsp;
debts and regrets. But here, he was a demi-god.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:22.320
A leader among the rest of us mere mortals. Why&nbsp;
would he ever want to go back to the world that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:28.560
had given him nothing and taken everything?
Day 70 to Day 84. We were forced into weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:33.360
of hard labor after that, toiling under&nbsp;
the Generalissimo and his gang of brigands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:38.400
The soldiers worked us like dogs, making us carry&nbsp;
food and furniture back to the Generalissimo’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:44.240
Scandanavian furniture fortress. One day bled into&nbsp;
the next. The best I could say about any of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:47.600
was that at least we were safe behind&nbsp;
the walls of the fortress at night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:51.040
so we didn’t need to worry about getting&nbsp;
murdered by the staff in our sleep.

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:57.360
However, tragedy struck again on day 85.
Kelvin couldn’t take the work anymore. One day, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:03.120
think his mind just snapped. He refused to follow&nbsp;
orders from the Generalissimo and his lieutenants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:08.080
even when they threatened him with death. Sadly,&nbsp;
that night they would prove that this wasn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.080 --> 00:17:13.040
some empty threat. When night fell, they tied up&nbsp;
Kelvin’s arms and legs and left him outside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:17.520
fortress. We were just forced to watch as the&nbsp;
staff assembled and beat our friend to death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:23.280
while he lay there, unable to defend himself. Even&nbsp;
in all the time I’d spent in the Infinite Ikea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:26.240
that was the most harrowing thing I’d ever seen.

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:30.240
But in one of the few acts of righteous&nbsp;
cosmic justice that we’d seen since being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:34.480
trapped in here, all those months ago,&nbsp;
just a few days after Kelvin’s brutal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.480 --> 00:17:40.880
execution. Day 90. The day of the revolution.
While I’d love to tell you I started this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:46.160
there are no heroes in this story, I just happen&nbsp;
to be the one telling you about it. Some internal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:51.120
conflict between the Generalissimo and his men&nbsp;
boiled over into a kind of civil war that tore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:56.000
the fortress apart from within. Vicky and I&nbsp;
escaped, but in different directions. I like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.000 --> 00:18:00.560
to imagine she got out in the end, it helps me&nbsp;
sleep at night. But one thing I will tell you:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:05.680
Generalissimo Vardagen found out what happened&nbsp;
to tyrants, big and small, when his closest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:10.320
confidantes gave him the Julias Caesar treatment&nbsp;
with the knives from which he took his name.

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:13.760
I don’t think there’s anything wrong&nbsp;
in taking a little joy in that.&nbsp;

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:18.240
For days afterwards, I just walked. I&nbsp;
felt so empty, but I refused to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:22.960
lay down and let myself die. Even though so&nbsp;
much of the hope had been beaten out of me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:28.080
I couldn’t betray Brenda by just giving up in&nbsp;
here. It wouldn’t be right. It just wouldn’t be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:35.760
right. The days ticked on, and nothing changed. I&nbsp;
had no food, no weapons. I was getting so tired.

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:41.600
Then… night fell, and the Staff started chasing&nbsp;
me. They seemed even more aggressive than before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:44.160
I couldn’t fight; all I could do was run.

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:48.880
I ran and I ran and I ran, not even looking&nbsp;
where I was going, as the crowd of staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:54.400
started catching up with me. Getting closer and&nbsp;
closer. I ran until there was a doorway before me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:58.800
I didn’t even think, I was just trying to get&nbsp;
away. That’s when I noticed that the store’s roof&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:04.880
was no longer above me. For the first time in one&nbsp;
hundred days, I was once again tasting fresh air.&nbsp;

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:11.040
That’s right, folks. On day one hundred,&nbsp;
I was out. I’d truly made it out.

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:16.000
This victory, however, was short-lived: A&nbsp;
group of about six staff members burst out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:20.720
of the front door behind me, charging towards&nbsp;
me with ferocious speed. I couldn’t move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:24.960
all I could do was grit my teeth and wince,&nbsp;
ready to accept my death at what had otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:29.520
been the high point of my recent life.&nbsp;
What a depressing irony that would be.

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:34.480
But instead, gunshots rang out through the air. A&nbsp;
hail of bullets cleaved through the staff members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:38.640
dropping them to the ground mere feet away from&nbsp;
me, stone dead. That’s when I turned to see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:43.200
group of men with assault rifles and tactical&nbsp;
gear walking towards me. In any other situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:47.520
this might have been terrifying, but right&nbsp;
then, it was the happiest moment of my life.

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:50.560
These men took me away from the&nbsp;
parking lot of what was now an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:54.800
abandoned Ikea. They told me they were&nbsp;
from a group called the SCP Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.800 --> 00:19:58.960
and that I’d been declared missing for some&nbsp;
time now. I didn’t care about any of that:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:04.240
I just asked them if Brenda had gotten out. If she&nbsp;
hadn’t escaped too, then this was all for nothing.

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:08.320
You can’t even imagine my relief when they&nbsp;
told me that Brenda was never even trapped.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:10.800
She was the one who reported my&nbsp;
disappearance after I’d dropped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:15.120
off the face of the earth in what she thought&nbsp;
was a perfectly normal Ikea shopping session,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:20.480
before that building was shut down and&nbsp;
cordoned off under some less-insane pretense.

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:26.080
But Brenda was alive and safe, and I’d get to&nbsp;
be with her again. I have no shame in telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:30.800
you I cried, but I’d like to specify&nbsp;
that they were absolutely tears of joy.

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:34.800
The men from the SCP Foundation&nbsp;
told me that they’d give me medicine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:38.480
that’d help me forget all this&nbsp;
after I told them my story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:43.360
and there’s no part of me that has a problem&nbsp;
with that. Some things are better left forgotten.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:48.480
But before the SCP Foundation wipes it all from&nbsp;
my mind and I get to go and live with my beloved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:55.200
wife once more, this was how I survived&nbsp;
100 dreadful days in the Infinite Ikea.

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:59.680
Now go check out “How To Actually&nbsp;
Beat SCP-3008 The Infinite Ikea”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:06.000
and “Surviving Inside SCP-3008 The Infinite Ikea”&nbsp;
for more of this Swedish flat-pack hellscape!

